<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
     AresLauncher - the information "i" itself. Composed into a backdrop by ares_info_badge.xml;
     that file is what callers reference, this one is only its top layer.

     REVERSED (2026-08-20). This was an exclamation mark, drawn that way because the owner asked
     for one by name - "add another option in the top right with a ! icon" - and this file used to
     argue the case against an "i" at some length: that the lowercase i in a circle is the Android
     convention for App info *specifically*, while this control opens the whole context menu
     (shortcuts, App info, Uninstall, Widgets), so naming only one of those entries under-promises.

     The owner corrected it after living with it: "the menu icon on apps should be an !, its shoild
     be the i in a circle as the information icon". Their call, and the reasoning above was in any
     case the weaker half of the argument. An exclamation mark is the universal glyph for a WARNING
     - it is what sits on error dialogs and on a widget that failed to load - so on a tile it reads
     as "something is wrong with this app" rather than "there is more to know about it". Promising
     slightly less than the menu delivers costs far less than a badge that looks like an alert on
     every icon on the home screen.

     Only the glyph changes. The backdrop, both sizes and the centred composition are untouched, so
     it still matches the x on the opposite corner exactly - see ares_info_badge.xml.

     Drawn as a dot over a rounded stem, mirroring the exclamation's own construction: the stem is
     the same 10.5-unit rounded bar at the same 3-unit weight as the x's strokes, and the dot is the
     same r=1.6 circle, so the swap changes the meaning without changing the weight.
-->
